    "text": "{\n  \"anomaly_detected\": true,\n  \"confidence\": 0.35,\n  \"anomalies\": [\n    {\n      \"category\": \"bridged_nets\",\n      \"components\": [\"U1\", \"U3\"],\n      \"pins\": [\"U1.1\", \"U3.1\", \"U1.2\", \"U3.2\", \"U1.3\", \"U3.3\", \"U1.5\", \"U3.5\", \"U1.6\", \"U3.6\", \"U1.7\", \"U3.7\", \"U1.8\", \"U3.8\", \"U1.4\", \"U3.4\"],\n      \"nets\": [\"NET0004\", \"NET0005\", \"NET0009\", \"NET0007\", \"NET0001\", \"NET0003\", \"+5V\", \"GND\"],\n      \"observed\": \"Two different MCUs, U1 (ATtiny13V-10S, SOIC-8) and U3 (ATtiny45-20P, DIP-8), are both placed with every pin on the same net as its counterpart. Both are powered from +5V and GND, and both drive PB0, PB1, PB2, PB3, PB4 and RESET on shared nets. The netlist carries no do-not-populate marking to show they are alternates.\",\n      \"expected\": \"Only one controller should be electrically present. If both footprints are intended as alternates, one must be marked DNP or kept out of the populated BOM. Otherwise two push-pull outputs fight on each shared pin and the board's behaviour is undefined.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U1\\\") (pin \\\"6\\\") (pinfunction \\\"PB1\\\") (pintype \\\"bidirectional\\\")))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U3\\\") (pin \\\"6\\\") (pinfunction \\\"PB1\\\") (pintype \\\"bidirectional\\\")))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U1\\\") (pin \\\"8\\\") (pinfunction \\\"VCC\\\") (pintype \\\"power_in\\\"))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U3\\\") (pin \\\"8\\\") (pinfunction \\\"VCC\\\") (pintype \\\"power_in\\\"))\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"If both chips are populated, they drive the same I/O pins and the ISP lines, causing output contention, unreliable fan control and possible overcurrent in the pin drivers.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.35\n    },\n    {\n      \"category\": \"missing_component\",\n      \"components\": [\"U2\", \"C3\"],\n      \"pins\": [\"U2.2\", \"U2.1\", \"C3.1\", \"C3.2\"],\n      \"nets\": [\"+5V\", \"GND\"],\n      \"observed\": \"The LM1117-5.0 output (U2.2, VO) on +5V is decoupled only by C3, a 100 nF 0805 capacitor. No bulk or tantalum output capacitor is present.\",\n      \"expected\": \"LM1117 regulators need a larger output capacitor, typically about 10 \u00b5F, for stable regulation. Without it the regulator can oscillate under load.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U2\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"VO\\\") (pintype \\\"power_out\\\"))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(value \\\"100nF\\\")\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"The 5 V rail may be unstable or oscillate when loaded, which would disturb the MCUs and the fan drive.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.15\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The netlist shows two different ATtiny parts (U1 and U3) wired in parallel on every shared signal and supply net with no DNP marking, which would cause bus contention if both are populated. The regulator output also has only 100 nF of decoupling, which is a weaker concern. The passive values and the FET and transistor switching paths look consistent with their roles.\"\n}",
